safix::value_spans_lines

  × the value for 'safix/ana/grafana' carries a newline, and the store's own
  │ command reads
  │ an entry's password as one line. Nothing was written.
  │ 
  │ What would land is the bytes before the first newline, which is a mirror
  │ that lies about what it holds — and the comparison that decides whether a
  │ run has anything to do is byte-exact, so a value ending in a newline would
  │ differ from the stored one on every run and rewrite the whole database
  │ each
  │ time, forever.
  │ 
  │ Nothing here strips the byte for you. `echo` appends a newline and
  │ `printf`
  │ does not, so a value minted or typed with one is re-established without:
  │ 
  │     printf '%s' "$VALUE" | safix set <user> <name>
  │ 
  │ or, where a generator minted it, change the generator's last write to
  │ `printf` and re-run `safix generate --regenerate <user> <name>`.
